Here are the Docker commands to remove containers and images.

## 1. Remove containers

```
# List running containers
docker ps

# List all containers
docker ps -a

# Stop a container
docker stop <container_id>

# Remove a stopped container
docker rm <container_id>

# Force remove a running or stopped container
docker rm -f <container_id>

# Remove all stopped containers
docker container prune
```

## 2. Remove Docker images

```
# List images
docker images

# Remove an image
docker rmi <image_id>

# Force remove an image
docker rmi -f <image_id>

# Remove dangling images
docker image prune

# Remove all unused images
docker image prune -a
```

## 3. Clean up everything unused

```
# Remove stopped containers, unused networks,
# dangling images, and build cache
docker system prune

# Also remove all unused images
docker system prune -a

# Also remove unused volumes (be careful: data can be lost)
docker system prune -a --volumes
```

## 4. Remove all containers and images

Warning: These commands delete all containers and images, including ones unrelated to your project.

```
# Stop all running containers
docker stop $(docker ps -q)

# Remove all containers
docker rm -f $(docker ps -aq)

# Remove all images
docker rmi -f $(docker images -aq)
```

## 5. For your Node.js monorepo

If you're using Docker Compose, you can clean up only your project's containers and network:

```
docker compose down
```

To remove the project's images as well:

```
docker compose down --rmi all
```

To remove its named and anonymous volumes too:

```
docker compose down --rmi all --volumes
```